  - chinese
---

> The secret to achieving that signature crispy yet tender pork is the double-fry technique. First fry at lower temperature to cook through, then a high-temperature second fry to create that restaurant-style crisp shell that holds up in the sauce.

== Prepare the Pork ==

Pound @pork tenderloin{280%g} lightly with a cleaver to tenderize. Cut into thin strips about 1 cm thick.

Rinse the pork strips and drain well, removing excess moisture. Set aside.

Finely chop @garlic{2%cloves} and @ginger{10%g}.

== Marinate the Pork ==

In a bowl, combine the pork strips with:
- @soy sauce{15%ml}
- @salt{1%pinch}
- @white pepper{2%g}
- @Shaoxing wine{15%ml}
- @baking soda{2%g}
- @egg white{1}

Mix well and refrigerate for ~{30%minutes}.

== Make the Sauce ==

In a separate bowl, combine:
- @white sugar{30%g}
- @tomato sauce{45%ml}
- @white vinegar{45%ml}
- @salt{1%pinch}
- @orange peel{1%strip} (optional)

Set aside. Reserve 30%ml of the starchy water for later.

== Make the Batter ==

In another bowl, whisk together:
- @all-purpose flour{50%g}
- @baking powder{3%g}
- @water{45%ml}
- @egg yolk{1}

The batter should be smooth and slightly thick. Add the marinated pork strips and let rest for ~{10-15%minutes}.

== Double-Fry the Pork ==

Heat @vegetable oil{100%g} in a #wok{} to 190°C.

Coat each pork strip in the batter and fry in batches until golden, about ~{2%minutes}. Remove and drain on paper towels.

Increase the oil temperature to 220°C. Fry the pork strips again for ~{1%minute} until extra crispy. Remove and set aside.

== Assemble ==

In a clean wok over medium heat, add a small amount of oil. Sauté the garlic and ginger for ~{2-3%minutes} until fragrant.

Pour in the sweet and sour sauce mixture and the reserved starchy water. Stir well and cook until the sauce thickens slightly.

Add the fried pork strips and toss to coat evenly in the sauce.

Garnish with @sesame seeds{5%g} and sliced @scallions{20%g}. Serve immediately.